  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.4 x^2 + 0.5 z^2)/2, drawn as a surface at height 0.5 × loss with contours. Steeper along x, so overshoot shows there first. Each x step multiplies x by 1 − 2.4 × rate (ignoring noise), so swings grow once the rate passes 2/2.4 ≈ 0.83.",
    "Noise is added to both slope parts: Gaussian with standard deviation 1.2 × sqrt((64 − B)/(63 B)), as when sampling B of 64 points without replacement. Exactly zero at B = 64. A step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "Both arrows are steps (rate × slope estimate, pointing the way the ball moves), drawn at 3 times their true length, one scale for both. Mini-batch step is the noisy step; Full-data step uses the true slope.",
    "Status: running or paused, finished after 40 steps, diverged when the loss goes past 5. Off-bowl points are drawn at the rim; Loss keeps the true value.",
    "The trail holds the start point plus one point per step (up to 41). The noise comes from a fixed seed, so Reset replays it. New run uses the next noise values and keeps the finished path as the ghost.",
    "The ball takes 5 steps per second at normal speed."
  ]
}